CAT = "s7Equity"; SEG = "EQU"
Q = [
("A shareholder owns 200 shares and four directors are up for election. With cumulative voting, the shareholder can cast:",
 "800 votes in any split", ["200 votes for each seat only", "200 votes in total", "800 votes for each seat"],
 "Cumulative voting multiplies shares by seats (200 x 4 = 800) and lets the holder put them all on one candidate. Statutory voting caps each seat at 200."),
("Which preferred stock feature lets the holder receive missed dividends before common stock gets paid?",
 "Cumulative", ["Participating", "Callable", "Adjustable rate"],
 "Cumulative preferred carries forward any skipped dividends in arrears, and those must be paid before common holders receive a dividend."),
("A stock's record date is Thursday, May 14. With regular-way T+1 settlement, the ex-dividend date is:",
 "Thursday, May 14", ["Wednesday, May 13", "Tuesday, May 12", "Friday, May 15"],
 "Since settlement moved to T+1 in May 2024, the ex-date for a regular cash dividend is the same business day as the record date. A buyer on that day settles after the record date."),
("A company offers holders one right per share, and 5 rights plus $40 buy one new share. The stock trades cum rights at $52. The right's theoretical value is:",
 "$2", ["$2.40", "$12", "$1.50"],
 "Cum rights value = (market - subscription) / (rights needed + 1) = (52 - 40) / (5 + 1) = $2. Dividing by 5 gives $2.40, the ex-rights formula, which applies only once the stock trades ex rights."),
("Which describes a warrant?",
 "A long-term right to buy above market", ["A short-term right to buy below the market", "A dividend paid in additional shares", "A preferred share with a fixed dividend"],
 "Warrants are usually issued with bonds or preferred as a sweetener, carry an exercise price above the market at issue, and run for years. Rights are short-lived and priced below market."),
("An American depositary receipt pays dividends in U.S. dollars. Its holder still faces:",
 "Currency risk", ["No foreign risk at all", "Unlimited liability", "Mandatory call risk"],
 "The underlying foreign shares pay in local currency, which the depositary bank converts. A stronger dollar shrinks the dividend and the ADR's value."),
("A company with 10 million shares outstanding buys back 1 million shares. The bought-back shares:",
 "Become treasury stock without votes", ["Remain outstanding and keep their votes", "Must be retired within one year", "Become authorized but unissued preferred"],
 "Repurchased shares are treasury stock: issued but not outstanding, with no vote and no dividend. Outstanding shares drop to 9 million, which raises earnings per share."),
("A customer holds 300 shares bought at $60. After a 3-for-2 split, the position is:",
 "450 shares, cost basis $40", ["450 shares, cost basis $60", "200 shares, cost basis $90", "600 shares, cost basis $30"],
 "A 3-for-2 split gives 1.5 shares for each one: 300 x 1.5 = 450 shares. Total cost of $18,000 is unchanged, so each share's basis is $40."),
("A convertible preferred has a par value of $100 and converts into 4 shares of common. The common trades at $27. The preferred's parity price is:",
 "$108", ["$100", "$104", "$25"],
 "Parity equals conversion ratio times the common price: 4 x $27 = $108. The conversion price is $25 ($100 / 4), which is a different figure."),
("Which preferred stock is most sensitive to rising interest rates?",
 "Straight, noncallable preferred", ["Adjustable-rate preferred", "Convertible preferred stock", "Participating preferred stock"],
 "A fixed dividend with no conversion or reset feature behaves like a perpetual bond, so its price falls most when rates rise. Adjustable-rate preferred resets with rates."),
("Which matter is normally put to a vote of the common stockholders?",
 "Electing the board of directors", ["Declaring a cash dividend", "Hiring the chief executive", "Approving the annual budget"],
 "Shareholders elect directors and vote on major changes such as mergers and new authorized shares. The board declares dividends and hires officers."),
("A REIT must distribute what share of its taxable income to keep favorable tax treatment?",
 "At least 90%", ["At least 75%", "At least 50%", "At least 98%"],
 "A REIT that pays out at least 90% of taxable income avoids corporate tax on what it distributes. Seventy-five percent is the asset and income test, not the payout."),
("REIT dividends paid to individual investors are generally taxed as:",
 "Ordinary income", ["Qualified dividends", "Tax-free return of capital", "Long-term capital gains"],
 "Most REIT dividends do not meet the qualified dividend rules and are taxed at ordinary rates. Some portions may be capital gain or return of capital, but ordinary income is the norm."),
("A stock pays a quarterly dividend of $0.75 and trades at $60. Its current yield is:",
 "5%", ["1.25%", "4%", "7.5%"],
 "Annual dividend = $0.75 x 4 = $3.00, and $3 / $60 = 5%. Using the single quarterly payment gives 1.25%."),
("Which feature lets an issuer force holders to give up preferred stock at a set price?",
 "A call feature", ["A conversion feature", "A cumulative feature", "A participation feature"],
 "Callable preferred can be redeemed by the issuer at the call price, which caps its price when rates fall. Conversion is the holder's choice, not the issuer's."),
("A sponsored ADR differs from an unsponsored one because:",
 "The foreign issuer takes part", ["It trades only over the counter", "Holders cannot receive dividends", "It is issued by the U.S. Treasury"],
 "In a sponsored ADR the foreign company works with the depositary bank and pays its costs, and holders usually get voting rights. Unsponsored ADRs are set up by a bank alone."),
("A rights offering is most likely to be made to:",
 "Protect existing holders from dilution", ["Pay a dividend to holders in cash", "Give outside investors a discounted price", "Reduce the number of shares outstanding"],
 "A preemptive rights offering lets current shareholders buy new shares in proportion to their holdings before outsiders, so their ownership share is not diluted."),
("Which is a key risk of holding common stock rather than the same company's bonds?",
 "Last claim in a bankruptcy", ["Fixed dividend payments each year", "Maturity date reinvestment risk", "Being called away at par value"],
 "Common stockholders are paid after all creditors and preferred holders in liquidation. Their dividends are not fixed, and stock has no maturity or par call."),
("A stock trading at $80 pays a 10% stock dividend. Assuming no other change, the price adjusts to about:",
 "$72.73", ["$72.00", "$88.00", "$80.00"],
 "Total value is unchanged and shares rise by 10%, so price = $80 / 1.10 = about $72.73. Subtracting 10% of the price gives $72, which overstates the drop."),
("A company has net income of $12 million, pays $2 million in preferred dividends and has 5 million common shares. Earnings per share is:",
 "$2.00", ["$2.40", "$2.80", "$1.67"],
 "EPS = (net income - preferred dividends) / common shares = ($12M - $2M) / 5M = $2.00. Ignoring preferred dividends gives $2.40."),
("Which describes a participating preferred stock?",
 "May earn more than its stated dividend", ["Must be converted into common stock", "Votes on all board matters equally", "Has a rate that resets each quarter"],
 "Participating preferred can share in extra dividends above its stated rate when the company does well. A rate that resets each quarter describes adjustable-rate preferred."),
("A rights offering's subscription period is typically:",
 "A few weeks", ["Five to ten years", "Indefinite", "One business day"],
 "Rights are short-term, usually expiring within about 30 to 45 days. Warrants are the long-dated instrument, often lasting years."),
("A customer has an open sell stop order at $45 on a stock that goes ex-dividend for $0.50. Unless marked do not reduce, on the ex-date the order becomes a:",
 "Sell stop at 44.50", ["Sell stop at 45.50", "Sell stop at 45.00", "Sell limit at 44.50"],
 "Under FINRA Rule 5330, open buy limit, sell stop and sell stop-limit orders are reduced by the cash dividend on the ex-date, so 45 - 0.50 = 44.50."),
("A customer buys shares of a closed-end fund on an exchange. The price she pays is set by:",
 "Supply and demand", ["The fund's NAV only", "NAV plus a sales charge", "The fund's sponsor each day"],
 "Closed-end fund shares trade like stock and can sell above or below NAV. Open-end funds are bought at the public offering price based on NAV."),
]
